  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to combine periodic optimization with multi-resolution analysis to improve upon the 0.380861 record. Periodic solutions are likely optimal due to the nature of the overlap integral, which involves shifts over a circular domain. We will: (1) use a hierarchy of resolutions starting from coarse and increasing granularity, (2) initialize with periodic Beatty sequences rather than uniform interpolation, (3) employ dimensionality reduction on periodic components, and (4) use adaptive active-set methods. The key limitation is that while period finders can identify irrational proportions, exhaustive searches over all periods would be prohibitively expensive. The approach balances global exploration with local refinement.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, n = resolution parameter\n- Objective: C\u2085 = max_{k\u2208[0,2]} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Constraint: \u222b h(x) dx = 1 (implementation: h_sum * (2/n) = 1)\n- Representation: piecewise constant on a periodic basis defined by Beatty sequence parameters \u03b1\n- Periodicity assumption: h(x+L) = h(x) for some base period L\n- Dimension reduction: decompose into components with wavelengths that are consecutive irrational numbers (e.g., golden ratio powers: (1+\u221a5)/2)\n- Score calculation: \n  C\u2085(h) = max_dx max(|{k: \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx > T}|) \n  then compute the maximum overlap value\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   a. Take parent as baseline if available, else create random binary vector\n   b. Generate multiple candidate periodicities using Beatty sequences with bases from a list of irrationals\n   c. For each base \u03b1 and resolution n:\n         positions = [floor(k * \u03b1) % n for k=1,N]  (ensuring uniform coverage)\n\n2. Core search (per resolution):\n   a. Use gradient-based optimization (SciPy L-BFGS-B) with:\n        - Box constraints 0\u2264h_i\u22641\n        - Nonlinear transformation of periodicity:\n            h[period_base:-period_base] = interpolate.antenor check \"shift\")\n        - Objective: surrogate of max correlation using Chebyshev smoothing\n   b. Implement adaptive gradient projection to enforce periodicity constraints\n   c. When improving a candidate, recompute C\u2085 for all shifts, not just one\n\n3. Dimensionality management:\n   a. Start with low-resolution full-space optimization (n=100)\n   b. Then transform solution to periodic form (split h across waves of lengths: 2\u03c0/\u03b1, with \u03b1 from {\u221a2, \u221a3, golden ratio})\n   c. Use spectral graph theory to identify promising harmonic components (von Neumann mixing)\n\n4. Resampling strategy:\n   - Base resolutions: 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600 (powers of 2)\n   - Compute C\u2085 only for hierarchical neighbors (using downsampling to 100-point window)\n   - Maintain a global best candidate and rebuild at full resolution\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n1. Start with Beatty sequences with \u03b1 \u2208 {\u221a3, golden ratio, \u03c0/e}\n2. After initial optimization, use grid search for embedding dimension 2 to 8\n3. When stuck, periodically rerun with new set of irrationals (hyperparameter)\n4. Use hierarchical Bayesian optimization to compare period candidates\n   - Variation 1: Alternate between full-space and periodic initialization\n   - Variation 2: Introduce controlled noise to periodic solutions to escape local optima\n   - Variation 3: Add Gaussian process-based surrogate for C\u2085 evaluation\n\nComputebudget androbustness:\n- Time allocation: 20% for initial Beatty generation, 30% for optimization per resolution\n- Check user.get_time_remaining() before expensive phase\n- Have fallback periodic scheme with smoothness parameter if optimization fails\n- Allow maximal n=1600 if over 400 in time (default budget allows three full-width runs)\n- Fallback periodic form: use Fourier basis coefficients limited to integer frequency gaps\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Verify 0\u2264h_i\u22641 for all i at return\n2. Confirm sum(h)*(2/n)=1\n3. Re-compute C\u2085 with numpy.correlate for top 20 shifts\n4. For returning help: only one representation needed (use periodic form offering smallest C\u2085)\n5. If periodicity-based solution not valid, project to simple grid search of dimension 8",
